umn-major? Let's think.\n\nCurrent baseline snake algorithm accesses columns strided across row vectors, terrible cache. Ring streaming with colSum reads each input value once and stores in ring rows sequentially; then horizontal max over contiguous colSum array. This is O(N^2) independent of S for window updates (except initial), much better than baseline O(SN^2). Should be significantly faster.\n\nNeed ensure correctness for all sizes/seeds/repeated calls: Input only one test case, no seeds. Repeated runs same input; parser static buffer okay because program exits after one call? If function called multiple times in same process? main once. Fine.\n\nPotential issue with `std::vector<int> rows(S * N)` when S*N could be 64M ints =256MB; plus colSum ~32KB, fine. But if memory limit lower (e.g., 128MB), baseline vector<vector> also uses >256MB for max, so judge likely allows. Could reduce to ring of S rows exactly as above.\n\nNeed include `<algorithm>`? We can use manual `if` instead of std::max to avoid overhead; but compiler inline anyway. Use `#include <cstdio>`, `<vector>`. No need iostream if using fread and printf, but maybe keep? Remove iostream for faster startup? Include only cstdio/vector.\n\nNeed implement parser carefully with static buffer:\n```\nstatic const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\nstatic char buf[BUFSIZE];\nstatic int pos = 0, len = 0;\n\ninline int nextInt() {\n    while (true) {\n        if (pos >= len) { len = fread(buf,1,BUFSIZE,stdin); pos=0; }\n        char c = buf[pos++];\n        if (c == '-' || (c>='0'&&c<='9')) {\n            int sign = 1;\n            if (c=='-') { sign=-1; \n                // need next digit\n                while (pos>=len) refill...\n                c=buf[pos++];\n            }\n            int x=0;\n            while (true) {\n                if (c>='0'&&c<='9') { x = x*10 + (c-'0'); }\n                else break; // but we already consumed delimiter? Need handle.\n            }\n...\n```\nBetter implement robust:\n```\ninline bool readInt(int &out) {\n    int c;\n    do { c=getcharFast(); } while(c<= ' ');\n    int sign=1;\n    if(c=='-'){sign=-1;c=getcharFast();}\n    int x=c-'0';\n    while((c=getcharFast())>='0'&&c<='9') x=x*10+c-'0';\n    out=sign*x;\n    return true;\n}\n```\nNeed `getcharFast` refill:\n```\ninline char getChar() {\n    if (pos >= len) { len = fread(buf,1,BUFSIZE,stdin); pos=0; }\n    return buf[pos++];\n}\n```\nBut when parsing digits, after reading non-digit we've consumed one delimiter; that's okay because next call skips whitespace. Need handle EOF: if len==0 return 0 maybe.\n\nImplementation:\n```\nstatic inline int readInt() {\n    char c;\n    do { c = getChar(); } while (c <= ' ');\n    int sign=1;\n    if(c=='-'){sign=-1;c=getChar();}\n    int x=c-'0';\n    for(;;){ c=getChar(); if(c<'0'||c>'9') break; x=x*10 + c-'0'; }\n    return sign*x;\n}\n```\nPotential issue: `getChar` returns char from buffer, at EOF len=0 and pos++ beyond? We only call when input valid. Fine.\n\nNeed ensure no use of `std::cin`, so remove iostream include to reduce overhead.\n\nLet's write full solution.cpp:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <vector>\n\nstatic const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\nstatic char buf[BUFSIZE];\nstatic int pos = 0, len = 0;\n\ninline char getChar() {\n    if (pos >= len) {\n        len = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n        pos = 0;\n    }\n    return buf[pos++];\n}\n\ninline int readInt() {\n    char c;\n    do { c = getChar(); } while (c <= ' ');\n    int sign = 1;\n    if (c == '-') { sign = -1; c = getChar(); }\n    int x = c - '0';\n    for (;;) {\n        c = getChar();\n        if (c < '0' || c > '9') break;\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n    }\n    return sign * x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n = readInt();\n    int s = readInt();\n\n    std::vector<int> colSum(n, 0);\n    // ring buffer of S rows\n    std::vector<int> rows(static_cast<size_t>(s) * n);\n\n    auto computeMax = [&]() -> int {\n        int cur = 0;\n        for (int c = 0; c < s; ++c) cur += colSum[c];\n        int mx = cur;\n        for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n            cur += colSum[j] - colSum[j - s];\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        }\n        return mx;\n    };\n\n    // read first S rows\n    for (int r = 0; r < s; ++r) {\n        int* row = &rows[static_cast<size_t>(r) * n];\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            row[c] = v;\n            colSum[c] += v;\n        }\n    }\n\n    int mx = computeMax();\n\n    // remaining rows\n    for (int r = s; r < n; ++r) {\n        int posRing = r % s;\n        int* oldRow = &rows[static_cast<size_t>(posRing) * n];\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) colSum[c] -= oldRow[c];\n\n        int* newRow = oldRow;\n        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            newRow[c] = v;\n            colSum[c] += v;\n        }\n\n        int curMax = computeMax();\n        if (curMax > mx) mx = curMax;\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", mx);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nNeed consider s could be 1; ring size 1, r%s works. For first S rows loop reads row 0 only; colSum=row values; compute max horizontal windows of width 1 (max element). Then remaining rows: posRing = r%1=0; subtract oldRow (previous row), read new row into same position, add; compute max. Correct.\n\nPotential issue with `computeMax` lambda capturing by reference and called many times; compiler may inline? Lambda likely inlined at -O3 but maybe not due non-static? Could define a function or macro for speed. Better write as static inline function taking pointers:\n```\nstatic inline int horizontalMax(const std::vector<int>& colSum, int n, int s) { ... }\n```\nBut vector reference overhead minimal. Or use raw pointer.\n\nNeed optimize computeMax to avoid lambda call overhead; compiler will likely inline because lambda defined in scope and called with known types? It may not if not marked `inline` but -O3 should. To be safe, define a separate function:\n```\nstatic inline int maxWindowSum(const int* col, int n, int s) {\n    int cur=0;\n    for(int i=0;i<s;++i) cur += col[i];\n    int mx=cur;\n    for(int j=s;j<n;++j){ cur += col[j] - col[j-s]; if(cur>mx) mx=cur; }\n    return mx;\n}\n```\nThen call `maxWindowSum(colSum.data(), n, s)`.\n\nNeed maybe unroll horizontal loop:\nCould use prefix sums to vectorize? Let's consider performance. The serial sliding loop has dependency on cur but each iteration is simple add/sub and compare; ARM can do ~1 cycle per iter if compiler pipelines? Dependency chain: cur += col[j] - col[j-s]; latency of int add maybe 2 cycles, so ~2 cycles/iter. N=8000,bottoms~8000 =>128M cycles ~0.04s at 3GHz; plus input parsing. Previous best 0.011s suggests benchmark smaller or optimized further.\n\nCould compute horizontal max using prefix sums with independent loop:\n```\nint pref[?] dynamic vector<int> pref(n+1);\npref[0]=0;\nfor i: pref[i+1]=pref[i]+col[i];\nint mx = INT_MIN;\nfor j=0; j<=n-s; ++j) {\n   int sum = pref[j+s]-pref[j];\n   if(sum>mx) mx=sum;\n}\n```\nSecond loop independent and can be vectorized with SIMD max reduction. But building prefix serial O(N). Total 2N operations vs N, but second loop may use NEON to process multiple j per cycle; first loop also maybe vectorizable? Prefix sum recurrence not easily vectorized without special techniques. Sliding window might be better.\n\nCould maintain horizontal sums incrementally across bottom rows too: Instead of recomputing all windows from colSum each row O(N), we could update previous row's window sums by adding new row contributions and subtracting old top row contributions, maintaining an array `win[j]` for current SxS windows. When moving down one row:\n- For each column c, colSum[c] updated.\n- Then win[j] = sum_{c=j}^{j+S-1} colSum[c]. Updating all j after colSum changes would require O(N*S) if naive; but can update using differences: newColSum[c]=old + delta[c]; then win[j]+=sum_{c=j}^{j+S-1} delta[c], which is sliding window over deltas, O(N). Same as recomputing from scratch? Recompute horizontal max only needs max, not all win; but to update max next row need know previous win values. Could maintain `win` array and update with delta windows:\n  - After updating colSum by adding new row and subtracting old row, compute delta[c] = addedRow[c]-oldRow[c].\n  - For each j: win[j] += sliding sum of delta over width S; can compute in O(N) using prefix or incremental.\nThen max over updated win. This is also O(N), similar to recomputing from colSum but maybe less operations? Recompute cur from scratch requires summing first S colSums (O(S)) plus N-S updates = O(N). Update win requires initial delta window sum O(S) + N-S updates = O(N). Same.\n\nCould combine reading and updating: While reading new row, we know added values; oldRow known. We can update colSum and also maintain `win`? Need compute horizontal max after full row; maybe not worth.\n\nPotential optimization: Use two separate loops for subtract old row and read/add to improve memory streaming:\n```\nfor c: colSum[c] -= oldRow[c];\nint* newRow=oldRow;\nfor c: v=readInt(); newRow[c]=v; colSum[c]+=v;\n```\nThis reads oldRow sequentially, then writes newRow same location. Good.\n\nCould avoid storing rows entirely by using column sums and a queue of row values? Need old row to subtract; ring is minimal.\n\nNeed think about input parser correctness with negative numbers: `readInt` skips whitespace including '\\r' etc. It treats any char <= ' ' as whitespace, okay. After parsing digits, it consumes one non-digit delimiter (space/newline) and discards; next call skip loop will consume further whitespace. Good.\n\nPotential issue at EOF after last digit: `getChar` may refill with len=0 and return buf[pos++] out-of-bounds? In readInt's for(;;), after reading last digit, it calls getChar to check next char; if EOF, our getChar sets len=fread(...)=0, pos=0, returns buf[0] uninitialized. That could be non-digit so break; okay but accessing uninitialized buffer is technically UB? `buf` static zero-initialized, so returns 0 (NUL), fine. But if fread fails and len=0, we still return buf[pos++] where pos was reset to 0 then increments to1; buf[0] valid zero. Fine.\n\nNeed maybe handle first line \"n groups=n\"? Wait problem B input is N S integers, not labels. Current baseline uses cin >> n >> s, so okay.\n\nNow need decide if this candidate likely improves over previous best. It changes algorithm from O(SN^2) to O(N^2), custom parser, flat ring buffer. Should be strong. Need ensure no hidden issue: The horizontal max computation after each bottom row computes all SxS windows for that top-left row. We do it for every bottom r>=S-1; number of bottoms = N-S+1. For each compute O(N). Total ~N*(N-S+1) plus reading N^2. Baseline snake also does O(S*N^2); ours much better when S large, same order when S small? If S=1, baseline snake: moving horizontally updates column sums of width 1 by subtracting/adding one element per step; each horizontal move O(1), vertical move O(1)? Let's analyze baseline for S=1: It maintains sum of current 1x1 cell. Moving right subtracts exitingCol (one value) and adds enteringCol, O(1). So total O(N^2). Our algorithm also O(N^2): reading N^2 plus horizontal max per row O(N), so ~2N^2; baseline maybe less constant but custom parser helps. For S small, ours may be slightly slower than optimized snake due extra horizontal loop? But previous best 0.011s likely already used similar ring+colSum and horizontal sliding, so okay.\n\nCould we further optimize for all S by using a hybrid: if S is large use our O(N^2) streaming; if S small maybe baseline snake with flat matrix? But one candidate only; keep simple.\n\nNeed consider memory allocation `rows(static_cast<size_t>(s) * n)` when s,n read from input. If s=0? Constraint 0<S<N, so okay. If malicious invalid, not needed.\n\nPotential issue: `int* row = &rows[static_cast<size_t>(r) * n];` vector indexing with size_t; fine.\nNeed include `<cstddef>`? Not necessary.\n\nCould use `malloc` instead of vector for speed? Vector initialization zero-initializes rows (256MB memset), which costs time. We don't need initialize ring buffer because we only read positions after writing them: first S rows write each position before later subtract; so no need zero init. But `std::vector<int> rows(size)` value-initializes to 0, causing O(N^2) overhead (memset). That could hurt. Use `std::unique_ptr<int[]>` or `malloc` to avoid initialization:\n```\nint* rows = static_cast<int*>(malloc(sizeof(int)*s*n));\n...\nfree(rows);\n```\nSimilarly colSum needs zero init; vector okay small N up to 8000, but can use calloc.\nUsing malloc avoids memset for large ring. Need include <cstdlib>. This is important for performance.\n\nLet's switch to raw pointers:\n```\nint* rows = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int) * (size_t)s * n);\nif (!rows) return 1;\nint* colSum = (int*)calloc(n, sizeof(int));\n...\nfree(rows); free(colSum);\n```\nNeed ensure no exceptions. Use `std::unique_ptr<int[]>` with `new int[size]` default-initializes? For fundamental types, `new int[n]` leaves uninitialized unless parentheses; so okay:\n```\nint* rows = new int[(size_t)s * n];\nint* colSum = new int[n](); // zero-init\n...\ndelete[] rows; delete[] colSum;\n```\nBut `new int[size]()` value-initializes to 0. For rows we don't want init, use `new int[size]` no parentheses -> default initialization leaves uninitialized for scalars? Actually `new int[n]` default-initializes each element, which for fundamental types means indeterminate (no initialization). Yes.\nUse:\n```\nint* rows = new int[(size_t)s * n];\nint* colSum = new int[n]();\n```\nNeed handle allocation failure? Not necessary but can check.\n\nHowever using `new` may call overhead; malloc similar. Use `malloc` for clarity and no init:\n```\n#include <cstdlib>\nint* rows = (int*)std::malloc(sizeof(int) * (size_t)s * n);\nint* colSum = (int*)std::calloc(n, sizeof(int));\nif (!rows || !colSum) return 1;\n...\nfree\n```\n`calloc` zero-initializes colSum.\n\nNeed ensure `s*n` fits size_t; N<8000 so max ~64M, fine.\n\nNow computeMax function:\n```\nstatic inline int horizontal_max(const int* col, int n, int s) {\n    int cur = 0;\n    for (int i=0;i<s;++i) cur += col[i];\n    int mx = cur;\n    for (int j=s;j<n;++j) {\n        cur += col[j] - col[j-s];\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n    }\n    return mx;\n}\n```\nCould unroll by 4:\n```\nfor (; j+3 < n; j+=4) { ... }\n```\nBut need handle tail. Might help ILP? The dependency chain still but multiple independent updates? Actually each update depends on previous cur, so no ILP across iterations unless we compute four separate running sums for different windows? Could use prefix approach.\n\nLet's consider using vectorized max via `#pragma GCC ivdep`? Not portable. Keep simple; compiler may auto-vectorize if we restructure with independent loop:\n```\nint pref[?] dynamic\npref[0]=0;\nfor i: pref[i+1]=pref[i]+col[i];\nint mx = INT_MIN;\nfor j=0;j<=n-s;++j) {\n   int sum = pref[j+s]-pref[j];\n   if(sum>mx) mx=sum;\n}\n```\nSecond loop has no dependency except mx reduction; compiler can vectorize with SIMD and use max instructions. First loop prefix serial but only N additions. Total 2N vs sliding N, but second loop maybe 4x faster due NEON, net similar or better. Also first loop could be optimized using pairwise? Not needed.\n\nCould compute horizontal max without storing full prefix by maintaining `win` array and updating with delta as above; still serial.\n\nGiven previous best ~0.011s, input parsing likely dominates. Our custom parser should help a lot. Need maybe optimize readInt further:\n- Use local pointer variables instead of static pos/len? Static function calls may inline.\n```\nstatic const int BUFSIZE = 1<<20;\nstatic char buf[BUFSIZE];\nstatic int idx=0, size=0;\n\ninline char gc() {\n    if (idx >= size) { size=fread(buf,1,BUFSIZE,stdin); idx=0; }\n    return buf[idx++];\n}\n```\n`gc` inlined. `readInt` uses while loops