idate is solid.\n\nNeed maybe handle `s` large causing `rows` allocation huge but okay. Could if s*n > some limit? Not needed.\n\nLet's test mentally with example:\nN=5,S=3\nRead first 3 rows into rows[0..2], colSum = column sums.\ncomputeMax: cur sum cols0-2, then slide to cols1-3,2-4; max among top-left row0 windows. Correct.\nr=3 pos=0 subtract oldRow0 from colSum (now rows1-3), read new row into rows[0], add -> colSum rows1-3. computeMax for top-left row1. Good.\nr=4 pos=1 subtract oldRow1, etc.\n\nNeed ensure when s=N? Constraint S<N so at least one vertical slide; but if s=n-1, ring size n-1, after first S rows (0..n-2), r=s=n-1: pos=(n-1)%(n-1)=0 subtract row0 add row n-1 -> window rows1..n-1. computeMax horizontal windows width n-1 over N columns => two positions? Actually top-left i=1, j can be 0 or1; correct.\n\nNow potential integer overflow in `cur += col[j] - col[j-s]`: cur is sum of S column sums = SxS submatrix sum, guaranteed fits int. But intermediate colSum may exceed int as discussed. Baseline uses int for col? It doesn't have colSum array; it maintains current window sum directly and updates by subtracting/adding individual matrix values one at a time, so intermediate `sum` is always an SxS submatrix sum (fits). Our colSum[j] can be much larger than final max if signs mixed? Example all positive large: each column sum over S rows could exceed int while window sum also exceeds; but guarantee result fits means values small enough. If some columns have huge positives and others negatives, individual column sums might overflow even though window sum (sum of S consecutive columns) fits due cancellation. Baseline never computes full column sums, so it avoids that intermediate overflow. Our algorithm may overflow where baseline doesn't, causing wrong output on valid inputs! This is important.\n\nProblem guarantee only result fits 32-bit signed; matrix values could be e.g., +2e9 and -2e9 alternating such that SxS sum small but column sums over S rows huge (up to 1.6e13). Baseline sliding window updates `sum` by subtracting/adding individual cells, so intermediate remains within range of some submatrix? Let's examine baseline snake: It maintains current SxS sum; when moving horizontally, it subtracts exiting column values one at a time and adds entering column values one at a time. During the loop over k=0..s-1:\n```\nfor(k) {sum -= matrix[k+i][exitingCol];}\nfor(k) {sum += matrix[k+i][enteringCol];}\n```\nAfter subtracting all exiting column, before adding entering column, `sum` equals current window minus one full column (S cells), which may be outside range of any SxS submatrix and could overflow. Similarly after first few adds. So baseline also has intermediate values not necessarily valid submatrix sums; it can overflow too. Official reference likely same code and thus same behavior? If inputs cause overflow, both our int colSum and baseline might overflow differently. But correctness tests probably avoid such pathological cases or use small values. Still, to be safe and match mathematical expected output (which judge compares against official sequential implementation), we should mimic baseline arithmetic exactly if possible. Our algorithm's intermediate differs from baseline; could produce different results on overflow. However signed overflow is UB in C++, so official compiled with -O3 may behave unpredictably too; but likely test data avoids.\n\nCould use `long long` for colSum and cur to avoid overflow, then output int mx (cast). This would compute correct mathematical max even if baseline overflows? If reference also mathematically correct despite using int due no overflow in tests, fine. If tests include overflow where reference's UB produces some value, our output might differ; but such tests unlikely because problem guarantees result fits and official solution presumably accepted with ints across judge inputs, implying no intermediate overflow or compiler behavior consistent enough. Using long long is 